On Wed, 18 Jan 2006 01:42:29 +0100, MackTuesday <mailbjl@yahoo.com> wrote:

> Is there a way to protect against polymorph traps that doesn't require
> a lucky find?  (Since yesterday I have discovered that among my rings I
> had polymorph control and searching.)

Ring of polymorph control would do find until you find something better.
You'll probably not be able to tell what "something better" is even after  
finding and identifying it, unfortunately. Enlightenment might help but  
the link between that property and "protect against poyltrap" is not  
obvious.

> If my stash becomes engulfed, will whacking the engulfer damage my
> stuff?

Not, but the monster may damage your stash while eating it.

> I also discovered yesterday that I shouldn't ever put a bag of tricks
> inside a blessed bag.  Do I correctly recall that there will be no
> problem if the containing bag is merely uncursed?  Are there any other
> items I want to keep outside a blessed bag?  (YANI: It would be cool if
> you could acquire an advantage of some sort by putting a blessed bag
> inside *itself* by means of some really weird trick, like putting an
> amulet of reflection inside it while hallucinating.)

Ever tried to #dip a potion into itself ?

As mentionned, it's not the fact that the bag is blessed, it the *nature*  
of the bag that doesn't fit with bags of tricks.
And with some other things.

> Can you push boulders while levitating?

No.

> What's the deal with smoky/swirly/cloudy potions?

Better drink them if they're blessed.
It's smoky only.
And milky (iirc) also might do something but that's far less important.

> I imagine that barbarians wouldn't be very good at writing, say, a
> spellbook of identify.  Is there anything I should know before
> attempting this?  Is there a spellbook flavor a barbarian should prefer
> to write or obtain?

You can only write scrolls/spellbooks you've already met (well, this is  
not exactly true, but not too far from true). You mainly want spellbooks  
that have spells associated to one of the magic school you're good at (the  
ones you can advance in the #enhance menu). Because #enhancing a spell  
school will brings more effect and success chances. And barbarians are not  
exactly known to be able to cast every spell they see...

> Is there a way to drag uncarriable monsters, specifically to altars?

Lure them on the altar and kill them directly where you don't have to drag  
them.



